   $q$Priya rolls a fair number cube 30 times and gets a 4 on 9 of the rolls. How much greater is the experimental probability of rolling a 4 than the theoretical probability?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$\\frac{7}{15}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"added-instead-of-subtracted"},{"text":"$\\frac{2}{15}$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$\\frac{3}{10}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"gave-the-experimental-probability-only"},{"text":"$\\frac{1}{2}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"subtracted-numerators-and-denominators"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$\frac{2}{15}$$q$, $q$The experimental probability is $\frac{9}{30} = \frac{3}{10}$ and the theoretical probability is $\frac{1}{6}$. Using a common denominator, $\frac{9}{30} - \frac{5}{30} = \frac{4}{30} = \frac{2}{15}$. The value $\frac{1}{2}$ comes from subtracting tops and bottoms separately, $\frac{3 - 1}{10 - 6}$, which is not how fractions subtract.$q$, 'draft', null)
