   $q$Each box of a cereal holds one of 5 different toys, and each toy is equally likely. Ana wants to design a simulation to estimate how many boxes a family must buy to collect all 5 toys. Which plan works best?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Spin a spinner with 5 equal sections until every number has appeared, record the number of spins, and repeat this many times.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Spin a spinner with 5 equal sections exactly 5 times and count how many different numbers appear.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"stopped-after-a-fixed-number-of-trials"},{"text":"Spin a spinner with 5 equal sections until every number has appeared one time, and use that single count as the answer.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-only-one-trial"},{"text":"Spin a spinner where section 1 covers half the circle until every number has appeared, and record the number of spins.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-non-uniform-model-for-equal-chances"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Spin a spinner with 5 equal sections until every number has appeared, record the number of spins, and repeat this many times.$q$, $q$Equal sections match toys that are equally likely, and spinning until all 5 numbers appear matches buying boxes until the set is complete. Repeating many times and looking at the results gives a reliable estimate. A single run can be far from typical, and a spinner with one large section does not model equal chances.$q$, 'draft', null)
